#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""cc-brief — the shape of a brief, checked by the box instead of remembered by the session.

  cc-brief check [FILE|-]        a brief on stdin/FILE -> the lines in it that prescribe HOW; exit 1 = refused
  …--for <repo>/<track>          names the track in the refusal (display only)
  cc-brief refresh <repo> <track>   recompute that track's IN FLIGHT block from the board, in its task.md
  cc-brief ready <repo> [<track>…] [--no-net]   which queued rows nothing in flight is holding, and which share
                                 a file. `--no-net` keeps it off GitHub, and it then answers less often
  cc-brief belongs <repo> <track>   the live rows already writing the files this row's brief names, how the ask
                                 reaches whoever is on one, and the command that adds it to that row's brief
  cc-brief selfcheck             fixtures for every signal, the four sentences the old judge cut, the recorded
                                 override and the row that is not one, the block rewrite, the ready arithmetic,
                                 and the join offer in both directions

WHY A CHECK AND NOT A RULE. What a brief may say is written down in two places already, and a session that had
read both still dispatched a page of prescription; a third copy is the move that has already failed twice. So the
three doors a brief enters by — `cc <repo> <track> --go "..."`, `cc-board add` and `cc-board set … instructions`
— ask this instead, the way the Stop hook asks cc-owed. All three, because gating two of them gates none: `set`
then `--go ""` puts a worker on the brief with no text left for --go to look at. Nothing here is a new copy of the rule: it is the rule's arithmetic.

WHAT IS REFUSED, and the count that decides it. Prose always passes — a message written to a person is most of a
brief and must dispatch as it stands. Three shapes do not:
  step-list      >= 3 list items that OPEN ON AN EDIT VERB (add, edit, run, create, change, replace, wire, …).
                 Numbering is not the signal: briefs list their done-criteria and boundaries numbered, and over 96
                 real briefs counting a numbered run as a sequence refused 18 of them.
  command-block  a fenced block holding >= 2 command lines — the exact commands to type, in order
  command-run    >= 2 bare command lines outside a fence: a whole line that IS a command, backticked or not
A line naming one command inside a sentence is not a finding; two lines that ARE commands is a script. A line
showing what a command PRINTED — column-padded, or carrying an arrow — is evidence, and evidence is a what.

PROSE THE ARITHMETIC CANNOT READ GOES THROUGH. A small model used to judge it and say what to cut; it struck
measurements and boundaries as often as method and was forced past most days, so it came out (shrink M3,
docs/scaffolding.md). The dispatcher reads its own brief; this file proves the shapes it can prove.
The sentences it cut are cases in the selfcheck now — an outcome, a boundary handing the design over, a done
clause and a score defined as a before and an after figure all pass — so nothing put back in its place may cut
them again; and a refusal here stays fixable by making the cut it quoted, which is what that judge was not.

FORCE IS FLAG, NEVER SILENCE, AND IT IS RECORDED: `CC_BRIEF_FORCE=1` in front of the dispatch prints the same
findings, lets it through (exit 0), and appends a JSON line to ~/.cc/state/brief/overrides.jsonl — countable
with `wc -l`, so an override is a deliberate act with a record, not a bypass.
A ROW THE BOX WROTE ITSELF IS NOT ONE OF THOSE. The landing's repair round is the box's own writing, not a brief somebody typed:
`CC_BRIEF_MACHINE=1` skips the check and writes nothing to the ledger, so the count above stays a count of
decisions people made.

THE BOUNDARIES ARE COMPUTED, NOT TYPED. What a brief needs to say about what is in flight — which files another
live track is already holding — is a fact the board and git both already have, and typing it means typing what
was true when it was typed. `refresh` reads the board's live rows (queued/running/waiting/review), asks git which
files each one's branch has changed against the base, and writes ONE delimited block into the brief:

  <!-- cc-brief:inflight -->
  IN FLIGHT (from the board, <stamp>) — held by another live track, so check before editing:
    core/bin/cc-foo — other-track [review]
  <!-- /cc-brief:inflight -->

Nothing held means no block at all, and an old one is taken out; a board that cannot be READ is not the same
answer, and leaves whatever the brief has. At most 12 files are listed, then a count and a pointer at the board.

Only that block is ever rewritten: everything a person typed in the brief is left exactly as typed, and a row a
person marked `kind=session` is not a track holding files, so it is read and skipped rather than reported.

WHICH QUEUED ROWS COULD START RIGHT NOW is the same arithmetic asked from the other end, and `ready` answers it.
Fifteen changes landed in seven hours on a box that still looked idle, because two or three builders ran at a time
while twenty rows sat queued: one writer per file is the right rule and is what caps the width, but it was read as
"wait" when the answer was usually "start it, nothing is holding its files". Nobody should have to work that out by
hand, so it is worked out from what is already written down — the board's rows, git's answer about each live
branch, and the paths each queued row's own brief names.
  A ROW'S FILES ARE THE ONES ITS BRIEF NAMES, and named() is the whole of that guess: a path that resolves in the
checkout, or a bare name that is exactly one tracked file (`cc-land`, `check.sh` — briefs write those constantly).
It errs toward collision: a file mentioned only as background counts. So this REPORTS and a session DECIDES — it
dispatches nothing, and it never edits a brief.
  …AND THE ROWS IT NAMES, which is the half a first version missed: briefs write "it goes after one-landing-queue
— it builds on it and cannot be written in parallel (same file)" and never spell the file, so the row read as free
while that PR held it. A brief that names another row inherits that row's files, held and named both.
  WHICH ROW HOLDS WHAT COMES OFF THE REF THAT IS REALLY THERE, not off the board's `branch` field, which goes
stale as a matter of course — a row cut on a branch of its own keeps the `track/<name>` it was created with. That
field naming nothing gave the same answer as a branch that had changed nothing, so a live PR held no files at all
and everything sharing them read as free. row_ref() tries the ref, then the row's own worktree (which has to be a
worktree of THIS repo — that field goes stale too, and a reused path would answer for somebody else's branch),
then the worktree git lists under the row's own name, then a fetched refs/pull/<n>/head, then GitHub.
  AND A ROW WITH A PR THAT NONE OF THOSE PLACE STOPS THE ANSWER RATHER THAN QUALIFYING IT. Its files are unknown,
so no row can be called free — free is a claim of safety, and the files that brief happens to name are a guess,
empty or not. The row is printed with its goal line so a person can place it: give it the branch the work is on.
That is why `gh` is asked by default and only about those rows — two seconds on a board of 105 — and `--no-net`
is for a run that must not leave the box. A ROW WITH NO PR AND NO BRANCH ANYWHERE HOLDS NOTHING, whatever its
status says: a project row an orch holds with its `agent` mark, whose code work runs in tracks of its own, is
one, and it withheld the answer for every queued row on the board until this was so (2026-09-12).
  TWO ROWS THAT NAME THE SAME FILE ARE ONE BUILDER'S WORK, not two, and that is the second half of the answer:
splitting them across two workers has cost this box a rebase and, twice, the same fix written twice. They come out
grouped, by the file they share, joined through it — a and b sharing one file and b and c another is one group.
  SILENCE WHEN NOTHING COULD START, like every other report worth reading: no free row, no output. A board that
cannot be read is not the same answer and also says nothing — not-knowing is never reported as nothing.

THE SAME ARITHMETIC ASKED OF ONE NEW ROW is `belongs`, and it runs at the door a row comes in by. Every row costs
its own landing — gates, a paid review read, a merge — so two asks in the same file that could have been one brief
buy a second landing for nothing. Whether they are one concern is a match a session makes from memory, and it is
missed exactly when the session has just changed hands. So it is made from what is written down instead: the files
this row's brief names, against the files every OTHER live row holds or names. Biggest overlap first, and NOTHING
when this work is nobody else's.
  IT IS GENEROUS WHERE `ready` IS CAREFUL, because the two claims are not the same. `ready` says a row is safe to
start and must not be wrong; this says "these two look like one piece of work" and a person decides. So a row's
files here are what git says it holds AND what its brief names, and a brief that names another ROW by name offers
that row whether or not either spells a file.
  BUT A FILE NAMED SO AS NOT TO BE TOUCHED IS NOT THIS ROW'S. Briefs write a boundary the way they write a target
— "core/bin/cc-land is held by PR #364", "the suites … do not touch them" — and reading those as ownership put
sixteen rows in front of a person, ten sharing nothing but a hub file, with the row worth joining thirteenth. So
a boundary clause is dropped whole, and one shared file only earns an offer when it is not a file the whole board
wants. Two rows wanting a file is the pair; a third live row wanting it means the file is where everything goes.
  WHO IS WRITING IT DECIDES HOW THE ASK LANDS, and the two sides of that are not symmetrical. A session running a
row in its own subagent can hand it the ask mid-run — and nothing on the box can see a subagent, so the row's
`agent` mark is the only evidence there is one. A headless worker cannot be reached at all: `cc --say` is refused
for one outright, and what it gets is its brief, at the next iteration. The offer says which it is rather than
leaving a person to find that out.
  IT OFFERS AND NEVER JOINS. Appending to somebody else's brief decides who is writing what, and a join is only
cheaper while the joined row stays ONE CONCERN — past the guardrail (~5-8 files) a review starts blocking on
volume and the saving inverts. So this prints the `cc board add` that would do it, and a session runs it or does not.
Env: CC_BOARDS · CC_STATE (both so a test run keeps out of the box's own state) · CC_BRIEF_FORCE · CC_BRIEF_MACHINE.
"""
import calendar, json, os, re, shlex, subprocess, sys, time

HOME = os.path.expanduser("~")
BOARDS = os.environ.get("CC_BOARDS") or os.path.expanduser("~/.cc/boards")
STATE = os.environ.get("CC_STATE") or os.path.expanduser("~/.cc/state")


def _board():
    """cc-board, imported the way cc-time is — beside THIS copy, not a stubbed BIN. It owns the board's opener and,
    since 2026-09-11, the brief's one locked writer; both are reached through here."""
    if not hasattr(_board, "mod"):
        import importlib.machinery, importlib.util
        s = importlib.util.spec_from_loader("ccboard", importlib.machinery.SourceFileLoader(
            "ccboard", os.path.join(os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(__file__)), "cc-board")))
        _board.mod = importlib.util.module_from_spec(s); s.loader.exec_module(_board.mod)
    return _board.mod
def board_read(path):
    """A board file, read through cc-board's opener: a member's board is a link into a directory the MEMBER writes,
    and a plain open here followed whatever it planted — a pipe hung this tool for good (review of #224). None = no
    board there; cc-board's Refused for anything planted, which a caller treats as unreadable."""
    return _board().read(path)
LIVE = ("queued", "running", "waiting", "review")   # a row that is not finished: its branch may be under a hand right now
DUE = ("queued", "blocked", "todo")   # a row WAITING to be picked up; `todo` is older boards' spelling of queued
MARK_A, MARK_B = "<!-- cc-brief:inflight -->", "<!-- /cc-brief:inflight -->"
BLOCK_RE = re.compile(re.escape(MARK_A) + r".*?" + re.escape(MARK_B) + r"\n?", re.S)
MAX_FILES = 12          # a brief carrying a page of paths is the problem this is here to avoid
MAX_QUOTE = 4           # findings quote the offending lines; enough to recognise them, not the whole brief

# An imperative opener. A bullet of prose starts on a noun or an article ("- the owner caught it"); a step starts
# on one of these ("- Add a flag to cc-foo"). Kept to verbs that name an EDIT, not verbs of judgement (decide,
# check, keep) — a brief may legitimately say what to be sure of, it may not say which keys to press.
IMPERATIVE = r"""add|append|edit|change|create|write|run|call|copy|move|delete|remove|rename|replace|set|make|use|
    put|open|insert|update|implement|refactor|wire|import|define|extend|rewrite|drop|register|export|install|patch"""
def _alt(words):
    """A wrapped word list -> one alternation. Splitting on whitespace alone leaves the wrap's trailing `|`,
    and the empty alternative it makes matches at position 0 of EVERY line — the check would fire on prose."""
    return "|".join(w for w in re.split(r"[|\s]+", words) if w)
IMPERATIVE_RE = re.compile(r"^(?:%s)\b" % _alt(IMPERATIVE), re.I)
LIST_RE = re.compile(r"^\s{0,6}(?:\d{1,2}[.)]|[-*+•])\s+(.*\S)\s*$")
FENCE_RE = re.compile(r"^\s*(?:```|~~~)")
# A command word: this box's own tools, and the shell things a prescription reaches for.
CMD_WORD = r"""cc|git|bash|sh|zsh|python|python3|pip|pip3|npm|npx|node|make|cmake|sudo|systemctl|journalctl|tmux|
    jq|yq|sed|awk|grep|rg|find|xargs|curl|wget|ssh|scp|rsync|docker|gh|pytest|cargo|go|ls|cat|head|tail|cd|mkdir|
    rm|cp|mv|chmod|chown|touch|ln|export|source|echo|printf|tee|diff|patch|kill|pkill|pgrep"""
CMD_RE = re.compile(r"^(?:(?:%s)|cc-[a-z][a-z0-9-]*|\./\S+|\S+\.(?:sh|py))(?:\s+\S+)+$" % _alt(CMD_WORD))
SENTENCE_END = re.compile(r"[.,;:!?]$|\.\s")   # a command line has no sentence punctuation; an English line does
# EVIDENCE IS NOT AN INSTRUCTION. Briefs show what a command ALREADY printed, in columns: `git ls-remote …  -> 79fd93b
# (STALE)`. Both real briefs this check refused across 96 were that shape. A command to type is single-spaced and has
# no arrow; column padding or an arrow means the line is reporting a result, which is exactly the what a brief is for.
EVIDENCE = re.compile(r"\S\s{2,}\S|->|=>|⇒")


def strip_block(text):
    """The IN FLIGHT block is this tool's own writing (paths, track names) — never check it as if a person typed it."""
    return BLOCK_RE.sub("", text)


def command_line(line):
    """Is this whole line a command? Backticks stripped; anything punctuated like English is prose naming a tool."""
    s = line.strip()
    if s.startswith("`") and s.endswith("`") and len(s) > 2:
        s = s[1:-1].strip()
    elif "`" in s:
        return False              # a command quoted mid-sentence is prose; only a whole backticked line counts
    if not s or SENTENCE_END.search(s) or EVIDENCE.search(s):
        return False
    return bool(CMD_RE.match(s))


def findings(text):
    """Every way this brief prescribes HOW: (signal, why, the lines), step-list first then the command shapes.
    The brief's own line — 'a brief carrying step lists or exact commands is refused or flagged at dispatch,
    with the reason' — is why each finding carries its own lines rather than just a count."""
    lines = strip_block(text).splitlines()
    out, fenced, fence_cmds, blocks, steps, cmds, fcmds = [], False, 0, [], [], [], []
    for line in lines:
        if FENCE_RE.match(line):
            if fenced and fence_cmds >= 2:
                blocks.append(fence_cmds)
            fenced, fence_cmds = (not fenced), 0
            continue
        if fenced:
            if command_line(line):
                fence_cmds += 1
                fcmds.append(line.strip())
            continue
        m = LIST_RE.match(line)
        if m:
            body = m.group(1)
            if IMPERATIVE_RE.match(re.sub(r"^[*_`]+", "", body)):
                steps.append(line.strip())
        elif command_line(line):
            cmds.append(line.strip())
    if fenced and fence_cmds >= 2:      # an unclosed fence still showed its commands
        blocks.append(fence_cmds)

    # NUMBERING IS NOT THE SIGNAL, the verb is. Briefs enumerate their done-criteria and their boundaries as
    # numbered lists all the time, and those are what the brief is FOR; measured over 96 real briefs, counting a
    # numbered run as a sequence refused 18 of them. An item that opens on an edit verb is a step in either list.
    if len(steps) >= 3:
        out.append(("step-list", f"{len(steps)} list items that are instructions, not a goal", steps[:MAX_QUOTE]))
    if blocks:
        out.append(("command-block", f"{len(blocks)} fenced block(s) of exact commands", fcmds[:MAX_QUOTE]))
    elif len(cmds) >= 2:   # a fence already said it: one finding for one brief, not the same commands counted twice
        out.append(("command-run", f"{len(cmds)} lines that are commands to type, in order", cmds[:MAX_QUOTE]))
    return out


def machine():
    """A row the box wrote for itself is not a brief a person typed: no gate to pass, and no override to record.
    It takes precedence over CC_BRIEF_FORCE, which is a PERSON deciding to send a refused brief as typed —
    counting an automatic row as one of those would make `wc -l` on the ledger a number about nobody."""
    return os.environ.get("CC_BRIEF_MACHINE") == "1"


def check(text, for_=""):
    """Print what prescribes HOW. Returns the exit code: 1 = refused, 0 = clean, or forced and so only flagged."""
    if machine(): return 0
    fs = findings(text)
    forced = os.environ.get("CC_BRIEF_FORCE") == "1"
    if not fs:
        return 0
    who = f" for {for_}" if for_ else ""
    head = "cc-brief: FLAGGED (CC_BRIEF_FORCE=1)" if forced else "cc-brief: REFUSED"
    print(f"{head}{who} — this brief says how, not what:", file=sys.stderr)
    for sig, why, ls in fs:
        print(f"  {sig}: {why}", file=sys.stderr)
        for line in ls:
            print(f"      {line}", file=sys.stderr)
    if forced:
        record(OVERRIDES, ",".join(sig for sig, _, _ in fs), for_, text)
        return 0
    print("  Say the goal, why it matters, the boundaries and what done looks like; the worker designs the rest.\n"
          "  If it really must carry this, CC_BRIEF_FORCE=1 sends it as typed (that is recorded).", file=sys.stderr)
    return 1


def changed(path, base, branch):
    """Files that branch has changed against base, per git. A branch that is not there has changed nothing."""
    for ref in (base, "origin/" + base):
        try:
            r = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", path, "diff", "--name-only", f"{ref}...{branch}"],
                               capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=20)
        except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
            return []
        if r.returncode == 0:
            return [f for f in r.stdout.splitlines() if f.strip()]
    return []


def board_of(repo):
    """The repo's board, or None when it cannot be read at all. NOT-KNOWING IS NOT NOTHING, and every caller
    below turns on that difference, so the two answers are separated here once."""
    try:
        return board_read(os.path.join(BOARDS, repo + ".json"))
    except Exception:
        return None


PR_NUM = re.compile(r"(\d+)\s*$")


def pr_number(pr):
    """The number a row's `pr` field carries. Rows hold both shapes — `225` and the whole URL — so both read."""
    m = PR_NUM.search(str(pr or "").strip().rstrip("/"))
    return m.group(1) if m else ""


def ref_names(path):
    """Every ref this checkout has, in each form a board row might name one: the full `refs/…`, a branch without
    its `refs/heads/`, and a remote-tracking name without its `refs/remotes/`. ONE call for the whole board —
    asking git row by row cost three processes a row on a loop that runs over every repo every couple of hours,
    and this file's whole claim is that it is cheap enough to run without anyone weighing it."""
    try:
        r = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", path, "for-each-ref", "--format=%(refname)"],
                           capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=30)
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
        return set()
    if r.returncode != 0:
        return set()
    out = set()
    for ln in r.stdout.splitlines():
        n = ln.strip()
        if not n:
            continue
        out.add(n)
        for prefix in ("refs/heads/", "refs/remotes/"):
            if n.startswith(prefix):
                out.add(n[len(prefix):])
    return out


def common_dir(path):
    """The git directory a checkout SHARES with its linked worktrees, absolute. "" when it is not a checkout."""
    try:
        r = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", path, "rev-parse", "--git-common-dir"],
                           capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=20)
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
        return ""
    d = (r.stdout or "").strip() if r.returncode == 0 else ""
    return os.path.realpath(os.path.join(path, d)) if d else ""


def worktrees(path):
    """{basename: branch} over the checkouts git itself lists for this repository — ONE call for the whole board.
    The board's `worktree` field goes stale the same way its `branch` does, and a row's worktree is cut at
    ~/.cc/worktrees/<repo>/<row> by convention (the watchdog flags one that is not), so the row's own NAME finds it
    off git's list without trusting either field. A detached or bare entry names no branch and is left out."""
    try:
        r = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", path, "worktree", "list", "--porcelain"],
                           capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=20)
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
        return {}
    if r.returncode != 0:
        return {}
    out, where = {}, ""
    for ln in r.stdout.splitlines():
        if ln.startswith("worktree "):
            where = os.path.basename(ln[len("worktree "):].rstrip("/"))
        elif ln.startswith("branch refs/heads/") and where:
            out[where] = ln[len("branch refs/heads/"):]
    return out


def row_ref(path, refs, row, name, git_dir="", wts=None):
    """The ref in THIS checkout that actually carries a row's work, or "" when no local ref does.

    THE `branch` FIELD GOES STALE, and that is normal rather than a fault: a row is created with `track/<name>`
    and the work is often cut on a branch of its own, so the field names a ref that was never pushed. Reading it
    and stopping there is what made a live PR hold NO files at all — the ref did not resolve, changed() returned
    the empty list, and every queued row that shares that PR's files read as free (review of #367). Three local
    answers, most certain first, and NONE of them touches the network: the ref itself, the row's own worktree
    (which is on the real branch, whatever the board says), and refs/pull/<n>/head when somebody has fetched it.
    Then the worktree git lists under the row's own name (`wts`, from worktrees()), which trusts neither field.
    A row none of them places and that has a PR is UNKNOWN, and held_files says so rather than reporting it as
    holding nothing; one with no PR either has no branch anywhere, and holds nothing (see held_files)."""
    ref = row.get("branch") or f"track/{name}"
    for cand in (ref, "origin/" + ref):
        if cand in refs:
            return cand
    # THE `worktree` FIELD IS AS STALE AS THE `branch` ONE — the same row carried both wrong — and a directory
    # at that path is not evidence it is this repo's: a worktree removed and its path reused by anything else
    # would answer for a branch of somebody else's repository. One call settles both questions, because the
    # branch and the git dir it shares come back together: the head, and the checkout it belongs to.
    wt = row.get("worktree") or ""
    if wt and git_dir and os.path.isdir(wt):
        try:
            r = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", wt, "rev-parse", "--abbrev-ref", "HEAD", "--git-common-dir"],
                               capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=20)
            out = (r.stdout or "").splitlines() if r.returncode == 0 else []
        except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
            out = []
        head = out[0].strip() if len(out) > 1 else ""
        shared = os.path.realpath(os.path.join(wt, out[1].strip())) if len(out) > 1 else ""
        if head and head != "HEAD" and head in refs and shared == git_dir:
            return head
    by_name = (wts or {}).get(name, "")
    if by_name and by_name in refs:
        return by_name
    pr = pr_number(row.get("pr"))
    if pr and f"refs/pull/{pr}/head" in refs:
        return f"refs/pull/{pr}/head"
    return ""


def pr_files(path, pr):
    """The files a PR changes, asked of GitHub. None = it could not say. THE ONLY THING HERE THAT REACHES THE
    NETWORK, and it is off unless the caller asks for it (`ready --net`): about a second a call, on a loop that
    runs over every repo every couple of hours, and this has to stay cheap enough to run without anyone weighing
    the cost. So the pulse's tick never makes it, and a row whose branch is gone and whose PR was never fetched
    is reported UNKNOWN there — which is true, and is what a person types `--net` to settle."""
    try:
        r = subprocess.run(["gh", "pr", "view", pr, "--json", "files", "-q", ".files[].path"],
                           cwd=path or None, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=15)   # a hung gh must not outlast the pulse tick
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
        return None
    if r.returncode != 0:
        return None
    return [f for f in r.stdout.splitlines() if f.strip()]


def held_files(board, skip=(), net=False):
    """({row: (status, [files])}, [the rows nothing could place]) — what each live track is holding, and the ones
    whose work no ref in this checkout carries. `skip` is the rows the caller is ASKING ABOUT: a brief is never
    told it holds its own files, and the rows `ready` weighs are weighed against what is in flight, not against
    each other. THE TWO ANSWERS ARE NOT ONE: a row holding nothing and a row nobody could read look identical in
    a file list, and reading the second as the first is how a PR held nothing at all.
    A ROW WITH NO BRANCH ANYWHERE HOLDS NOTHING, WHATEVER ITS STATUS SAYS. No ref, no worktree, no PR: there is
    no branch for a file to be on. A project row an orch holds with its `agent` mark is exactly that — its code
    work runs in tracks of its own — and reading it as UNKNOWN because it said `running` withheld the answer for
    all 23 queued rows at once, every pulse, until a seat wrote "none placeable" in its handoff and dispatched
    nothing (raised-one-branchless-row-makes-every-queued-row-unplaceable, 2026-09-12). Unknown is kept for the
    row that HAS a PR nothing local places: its work is somewhere, and GitHub is asked (`net`) or the answer is
    withheld. A branch pushed from any worktree of this repo is in `refs` as origin/…, so "no ref" is not stale."""
    path, base = board.get("path") or "", board.get("default_branch") or "main"
    refs = ref_names(path) if path else set()
    git_dir = common_dir(path) if path else ""
    wts = worktrees(path) if path else {}
    out, unknown = {}, []
    for name, t in sorted((board.get("tracks") or {}).items()):
        # kind=session is a word a person set: a channel with no task and no PR. Read it, honour it, never a holder.
        if name in skip or (t.get("kind") or "track") != "track":
            continue
        st = t.get("status") or ""
        if st not in LIVE:
            continue
        ref = row_ref(path, refs, t, name, git_dir, wts)
        files = changed(path, base, ref) if ref else None
        pr = pr_number(t.get("pr"))
        if files is None and net and pr:
            files = pr_files(path, pr)
        if files is None and not pr:
            files = []          # no ref, no worktree, no PR: no branch anywhere, so nothing to hold a file on
        if files is None:
            unknown.append(name)
            continue
        out[name] = (st, files)
    return out, unknown


def inflight(repo, exclude=""):
    """The IN FLIGHT block for repo, excluding one track (its own brief). Computed from the board, every time.
    Three answers, and they are not the same: the block · "" nothing is held right now · None the board could not
    be read, so nothing is known — and a caller must not mistake not-knowing for nothing and delete a real block."""
    d = board_of(repo)
    if d is None:
        return None
    # NO NETWORK HERE, unlike `ready`: this runs inside every dispatch, and a `gh` that is slow or logged out
    # must never be what holds one up. A row row_ref() cannot place locally is left out of the block, which is
    # what this has always done — the block is a pointer at the board, and `ready` is where being sure matters.
    byrow, _unplaced = held_files(d, skip={exclude} if exclude else set(), net=False)
    held = {}
    for name, (st, files) in sorted(byrow.items()):
        for f in files:
            held.setdefault(f, []).append(f"{name} [{st}]")
    if not held:
        return ""      # nothing is held: a brief carries no line about it, and a block left from before comes out
    stamp = time.strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%MZ", time.gmtime())
    rows = [f"  {f} — {', '.join(who)}" for f, who in sorted(held.items())[:MAX_FILES]]
    if len(held) > MAX_FILES:
        rows.append(f"  …and {len(held) - MAX_FILES} more (cc board show {repo})")
    body = f"IN FLIGHT (from the board, {stamp}) — held by another live track, so check before editing:\n" + "\n".join(rows)
    return f"{MARK_A}\n{body}\n{MARK_B}\n"


def refresh(repo, track):
    """The block, recomputed and put where the last one was through cc-board's locked writer (`annotate`): ONLY the
    delimited block moves, every line a person typed is left exactly as typed (self-review 6), and a note being
    appended or a re-dispatch landing at the same moment is serialised with it, not overwritten by it. Never a
    reason to fail a dispatch: no brief, an unreadable board, a held lock all leave the file as it was."""
    block = inflight(repo, exclude=track)
    if block is None:
        return 0        # the board could not be read: leave whatever is in the brief alone
    try:
        _board().brief_edit("annotate", repo, track, block, state=STATE)
    except Exception as e:      # a link planted at the file or the lock, a lock somebody holds: said, not fatal
        print(f"cc-brief refresh: {repo}/{track} left as it was — {e}", file=sys.stderr)
    return 0


# ── which queued rows could start right now ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
GOAL_QUOTE = 120      # how much of a blind row's goal line is quoted back: enough to recognise the row
MARK_H = 6            # CC_AGENT_MARK_HOURS, cc-pulse's and cc-reconcile's: how long a session's `agent` mark holds
# A FILE A BRIEF NAMES, and both shapes have to RESOLVE before they count — a brief is prose, and guessing at
# prose is how a report becomes noise nobody reads. A token carrying a slash is checked against the checkout; a
# bare one is looked up among the tracked files and counts only when exactly ONE file carries that name, because
# briefs write `cc-land` and `check.sh` far more often than they write the whole path. A bare token must carry a
# `.` or a `-` besides, or every English word in the brief would be asked about.
TOKEN = re.compile(r"(?<![\w./~@+-])[\w.@+-]+(?:/[\w.@+-]+)*")


def epoch(s):
    """A board stamp back to seconds; anything unreadable is long ago, so a mark nobody can date holds nothing."""
    try:
        return float(calendar.timegm(time.strptime(str(s), "%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ")))
    except (TypeError, ValueError):
        return 0.0


def tracked(root):
    """{basename: path} over the files git tracks in `root`, keeping only the names exactly one file carries.
    A name two directories share names NEITHER of them, and a checkout git cannot answer about names nothing."""
    try:
        r = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", root, "ls-files"], capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=20)
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
        return {}
    if r.returncode != 0:
        return {}
    idx, dup = {}, set()
    for line in r.stdout.splitlines():
        f = line.strip()
        b = os.path.basename(f)
        if not b:
            continue
        if b in idx:
            dup.add(b)
        idx[b] = f
    for b in dup:
        idx.pop(b, None)
    return idx


# A FILE NAMED IN ORDER TO SAY IT WILL NOT BE TOUCHED IS NOT THIS ROW'S FILE. A brief spells a boundary with the
# same words it spells a target — "core/bin/cc-land is held by PR #364", "core/tests/*.sh by the email-receiver
# track — do not touch them" — and counting those as ownership put ten unrelated rows on one hub file and pushed
# the row a person actually wanted off the end of the offer (review of #371). So a clause carrying one of these
# is dropped: every path in it is written down precisely because it belongs to somebody else. The IN FLIGHT block
# this tool appends says "held by another live track" and so falls to the same rule — belt as well as braces.
BOUNDARY = re.compile(r"do(?:es)?\s+not\s+(?:touch|edit|change|modify|write)"
                      r"|don'?t\s+(?:touch|edit|change|modify|write)"
                      r"|not\s+to\s+(?:touch|edit|change|modify|write)"
                      r"|never\s+(?:touch|edit|chang|modif|rewrit|writ)\w*"
                      r"|held\s+by|owned\s+by|hands?\s+off|leave\s.{0,40}?\balone", re.I)
# A BOUNDARY GOVERNS ITS CLAUSE, NOT THE WHOLE SENTENCE. Prose here is compound — "this row will edit
# core/bin/cc-land, but do not touch bin/cc-fixture" and "core/bin/cc-land is held by PR #364 and core/bin/cc-board
# is this row's to rewrite" say who owns what and who must stay off in one breath — and dropping the sentence took
# the row's real target with the boundary, under-reporting what it owns (re-review of #371). So a new predication
# starts at a semicolon, a newline, a full stop, an em-dash, ` and ` or ` but `.
#   NOT AT A COMMA, which is how a boundary writes its own object LIST: "Never edits cc-land, cc-guard, the gates
# (check.sh, check-extra.sh, selftest.sh)" is one boundary over six names, and splitting there would free five of
# them. The cost is the mirror case — "do not touch cc-land and cc-board" keeps cc-board — and that is the side to
# be wrong on: an extra offer is read and dismissed, a missing one is a landing bought twice.
SEG = re.compile(r"([;\n]|(?<=\w)\.\s|\s*[—–]\s*|\s+--\s+|\s+(?:and|but)\s+)", re.I)
SENTENCE = re.compile(r"[;\n]|\.\s")     # …and these END it, so nothing reaches back across one


def spells_something(seg):
    """Does this clause name anything that could be a file at all? Nothing here resolves it — the question is
    only whether a boundary brought its own object, or is leaning on the clause beside it."""
    return any("/" in t or "." in t or "-" in t for t in (x.rstrip(".,;:") for x in TOKEN.findall(seg)))


def owning(text):
    """A brief's clauses with the boundary ones dropped — what it says about its OWN work. Callers that ask
    "whose file is this" want this; callers that ask "is anything in the way" want the whole text, because a
    brief warning a row off a file is the strongest sign yet that the file is somebody's.

    A TRAILING IMPERATIVE TAKES THE CLAUSE BEFORE IT. "core/tests/*.sh by the email-receiver track — do not touch
    them" puts the boundary in one clause and everything it is about in the one before, and "them" can mean
    nothing else. So a boundary that spells no object of its own reaches back one clause — within its own
    sentence, never across a full stop, because "… is wrong. Do not touch." is two statements and not one."""
    parts = SEG.split(text)
    segs, before = parts[0::2], [""] + parts[1::2]
    keep = [True] * len(segs)
    for i, s in enumerate(segs):
        if not BOUNDARY.search(s):
            continue
        keep[i] = False
        if not spells_something(s) and i and not SENTENCE.search(before[i]):
            keep[i - 1] = False
    return "\n".join(s for s, k in zip(segs, keep) if k)


def named(text, root, index):
    """The repo files this brief names — the ones it names as ITS OWN, boundary clauses left out (see above).
    THE IN FLIGHT BLOCK IS NOT THE ROW'S OWN either: every path in it is somebody else's file, written there by
    this tool, so reading it as this row's would make every annotated brief collide with everything in flight —
    including itself. strip_block() takes it out, as it does before the shape check."""
    out = set()
    for clause in owning(strip_block(text)).splitlines():
        for tok in TOKEN.findall(clause):
            tok = tok.rstrip(".,;:")
            if ".." in tok.split("/"):
                continue      # a brief's `../` is somebody's shell line, and nothing outside the checkout is a
            if "/" in tok:    # file of this repo's — held_files only ever names paths git gave it
                if root and os.path.isfile(os.path.join(root, tok)):
                    out.add(tok)
            elif ("." in tok or "-" in tok) and tok in index:
                out.add(index[tok])
    return out


def bundled(row, rows):
    """Is this row inside a bundle that is live? cc-bundle puts several queued rows under ONE worker: the row it
    adds for them carries the brief, and each member's `bundle` field names it. The member stays `queued` — that
    is what lets the bundle's PR close it (cc-land's closer skips a running row) — so its status cannot say it is
    being written, and this field is what does. It holds only while the bundle row is live: a bundle that
    landed closed the members with it, and one that was abandoned or removed hands them back to the pool."""
    b = str(row.get("bundle") or "").strip()
    return bool(b) and b in rows and (rows[b].get("status") or "") in LIVE


def candidates(board, only=()):
    """The rows `ready` weighs: exactly the ones named, or every row waiting to be picked up. Three rows are NOT
    waiting even though `queued` is all any can say — one a person is deliberately sitting on (cc-board's
    `held`), one a live session has marked as its own subagent's work (`agent`, still warm), and one inside a
    live bundle (`bundle`, cc-bundle's). Offering any is offering work somebody is already doing or has already
    stopped. A caller that can test the mark's window properly — cc-pulse does, with tmux — passes its own rows
    in and the first two tests are not made; a bundled row is dropped even when named, because it is being
    written whoever asks."""
    rows = {k: v for k, v in (board.get("tracks") or {}).items()
            if isinstance(v, dict) and (v.get("kind") or "track") == "track"}
    if only:
        return {k: v for k, v in rows.items() if k in set(only) and not bundled(v, rows)}
    out = {}
    for k, v in rows.items():
        if (v.get("status") or "queued") not in DUE or str(v.get("held") or "").strip():
            continue
        if marked(v) or bundled(v, rows):
            continue
        out[k] = v
    return out


def marked(row):
    """Is a live session holding this row in its own subagent right now? NOTHING ON THE BOX IS THAT SUBAGENT —
    no process, no window, nothing to look for — so the mark the session made is the only evidence there is one,
    and it counts only while it is warm: a session that moved on without clearing it must not hold the row for as
    long as its window happens to live (review of #242). The same window every other reader of a mark uses."""
    cold = time.time() - float(os.environ.get("CC_AGENT_MARK_HOURS") or MARK_H) * 3600
    return bool((row.get("agent") or "").strip()) and epoch(row.get("agent_at")) > cold


def grouped(wants):
    """{row: files} -> [(the files they share, the rows), …] biggest first — the rows that belong to ONE builder.
    THE JOIN IS TRANSITIVE: a and b share one file, b and c another, and splitting those three across two workers
    puts two writers on b's files. Only groups of two or more come out; a row sharing nothing is nobody's group."""
    seen, out = set(), []
    for start in sorted(wants):
        if start in seen:
            continue
        team, files, edge = {start}, set(), [start]
        seen.add(start)
        while edge:
            k = edge.pop()
            for other in sorted(wants):
                if other in team:
                    continue
                shared = wants[k] & wants[other]
                if shared:
                    files |= shared
                    team.add(other)
                    seen.add(other)
                    edge.append(other)
        if len(team) > 1:
            out.append((sorted(files), sorted(team)))
    return sorted(out, key=lambda g: (-len(g[1]), g[1]))


def brief_text(repo, track):
    """A row's brief with the IN FLIGHT block taken out. That block is this tool's own writing and it names other
    tracks and their files — left in, every annotated brief would read as naming every row in flight."""
    try:
        with open(os.path.join(STATE, repo, track, "task.md"), errors="replace") as fh:
            return strip_block(fh.read())
    except OSError:
        return ""


def referred(text, names):
    """The other rows this brief names. A DEPENDENCY NAMED BY TRACK IS A COLLISION, and briefs say it that way
    far more often than they list paths: "it goes after one-landing-queue — it builds on it and cannot be
    written in parallel (same file)" names the row and never spells the file, so the row read as free while
    that PR held it (review of #367). One level deep: the row this brief names, and not what that row names."""
    return {n for n in names if re.search(r"(?<![\w-])" + re.escape(n) + r"(?![\w-])", text)}


def goal_of(text):
    """A brief's GOAL line, or its first line — enough for a person to recognise the row and go and place it."""
    lines = [ln.strip() for ln in text.splitlines() if ln.strip()]
    for ln in lines:
        if ln.upper().startswith("GOAL"):
            return ln[:GOAL_QUOTE]
    return lines[0][:GOAL_QUOTE] if lines else ""


def ready(repo, only=(), net=True):
    """(the rows that could start now, their groups, how many were weighed, the rows nothing could place, the
    ones of those that could not even be guessed at). None = the board could not be read, which is NOT the same
    answer as nothing could start.

    A ROW WITH A PR NOTHING COULD PLACE STANDS ON ITS OWN BRIEF. Its branch is gone and its PR was never fetched,
    so git cannot say what it holds; treating that as "holds nothing" is what let a live PR's files go unclaimed.
    The files its brief names stand in — a guess, and the caller is told which rows it was made for. A row with
    no PR and no branch anywhere is not that: it holds nothing (held_files), and never withholds the answer.

    …AND IT NEVER MAKES A ROW FREE. A stand-in is a guess about a row nobody could read, and FREE IS A CLAIM OF
    SAFETY: the row this was measured on holds two files its brief never spells, so a queued row naming one of
    them came back free with a general note that some row was unread as the only warning (re-review of #367). An
    empty guess and a partial one are worth the same here — neither can be stood behind — so an unplaced row
    withholds the whole answer. It can still take a row OUT of the free list, and it can never put one in.
    WHICH IS WHY THE NETWORK IS ON BY DEFAULT. Withholding is only useful if the ordinary run has something to
    withhold about: `gh pr view` is asked ONLY about the handful of rows no local ref places, measured at about
    two seconds on a board of 105 rows, and `--no-net` is there for a run that must not leave the box."""
    d = board_of(repo)
    if d is None:
        return None
    cand = candidates(d, only)
    if not cand:
        return [], [], 0, [], []
    byrow, unplaced = held_files(d, skip=set(cand), net=net)
    root = d.get("path") or ""
    index = tracked(root) if root else {}
    rows = [k for k, v in (d.get("tracks") or {}).items()
            if isinstance(v, dict) and (v.get("kind") or "track") == "track"]
    text = {k: brief_text(repo, k) for k in rows}
    own = {k: named(text[k], root, index) for k in rows}
    files_of = {k: set(v[1]) for k, v in byrow.items()}
    for u in unplaced:
        files_of[u] = set(own.get(u, ()))
    held = set().union(*files_of.values()) if files_of else set()
    wants = {}
    for k in cand:
        w = set(own[k])
        for other in referred(text[k], rows) - {k}:
            w |= files_of.get(other, set()) | own.get(other, set())
        wants[k] = w
    if unplaced:
        return [], [], len(cand), sorted(unplaced), sorted(u for u in unplaced if not files_of.get(u))
    free = sorted(k for k in cand if not (wants[k] & held))
    return free, grouped({k: wants[k] for k in free}), len(cand), [], []


def cmd_ready(repo, only, net=False):
    """Print the answer, and NOTHING AT ALL when there is none. Exit is always 0: this reports, it does not gate,
    and a caller reading it as a verdict on anything is reading it wrong. THE ROWS NOTHING COULD PLACE ARE
    PRINTED WITH IT — an answer that is partial and does not say so is the shape this whole file is against —
    and a row that could not even be guessed at withholds the answer instead of qualifying it."""
    r = ready(repo, only, net=net)
    if r is None:
        return 0
    free, groups, weighed, unplaced, blind = r
    if unplaced:
        # NOT A COUNT EITHER. "N could start, unread" reads as room, and the number is exactly what cannot be
        # stood behind: an unplaced row's files are unknown, so any of those N may be the one it collides with.
        one = len(unplaced) == 1
        print(f"{repo}: {weighed} queued row{'' if weighed == 1 else 's'}, none can be called free — "
              f"{'a row is' if one else f'{len(unplaced)} rows are'} in flight and nothing places "
              f"{'it' if one else 'them'}")
        for u in unplaced:
            goal = goal_of(brief_text(repo, u))
            where = "brief names no file either" if u in blind else "read from its brief only"
            print(f"  {u} ({where}) — {goal or 'no brief on file'}")
        print(f"  give {'that row' if one else 'those rows'} the branch the work is really on, or run this where "
              f"`gh` can reach GitHub")
        return 0
    if not free:
        return 0
    head = f"{repo}: {len(free)} of {weighed} queued row{'' if weighed == 1 else 's'} could start now"
    if groups:
        head += f" — {len(groups)} group{'' if len(groups) == 1 else 's'} share a file"
    print(head)
    together = set()
    for files, ks in groups:
        together |= set(ks)
        shown = ", ".join(files[:3]) + (f" and {len(files) - 3} more" if len(files) > 3 else "")
        print(f"  one builder for {shown}: {', '.join(ks)}")
    alone = [k for k in free if k not in together]
    if alone:
        print(f"  a builder each: {', '.join(alone)}")
    return 0


# ── which live row this new work belongs with ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
JOIN_SHOW = 3         # live rows offered; the rest are a count and a pointer at the board
# HOW THE ASK ACTUALLY REACHES WHOEVER IS WRITING THE ROW, which is the half a person cannot look up. The first
# route is the one nothing on the box can see: a session running a row in its own subagent hands it the ask
# mid-run, and the row's `agent` mark is the only sign that subagent exists. A headless worker is the other end
# of that — `cc --say` is refused for one in as many words — so what it gets is the brief, at its next iteration.
REACH = {
    "agent":   "a session has it in a subagent, which takes the ask mid-run — go through that session",
    "running": "`cc {repo} {row} --say` reaches it if it is a windowed session; a headless worker reads its brief "
               "at the next iteration",
    "review":  "its PR is open, so the ask goes in with it if it is added before the merge",
    "waiting": "it is stopped on a question, and the ask waits with it",
    "queued":  "nothing is running it yet, so the brief is the whole of it",
}
# ORDER, after the size of the overlap: the further along a row is, the more a join is worth here. An open PR is a
# landing already committed to, and a running row is work a person can be told about while it is still being
# written. TWO QUEUED ROWS SHARING A FILE ARE ALREADY SOMEBODY'S ANSWER — that is exactly what `ready` groups —
# so they come last rather than crowding out the rows nothing else reports.
ORDER = {"review": 0, "running": 1, "waiting": 2, "queued": 3}
HUB = 3               # live rows wanting one file before wanting it stops being evidence of anything
# WHY THREE. With a single shared file the whole offer rests on that one file, and this repo has a handful every
# row touches — cc-land, cc-board, the suites. Two rows wanting a file IS the pair, and that is the signal worth
# offering; a third live row wanting the same file says the file is where everything goes, so the two rows share
# the repo's common ground and not a concern. Below that floor an offer needs a second file to stand on.


def belongs(repo, track):
    """[(row, its status, how the ask reaches whoever is on it, the files both want)…] — the live rows already on
    this row's work, biggest overlap first. [] = it is nobody else's. None = the board could not be read.

    ONE SHARED HUB FILE IS NOT A CONCERN IN COMMON. Offering on any single file at all put sixteen rows in front
    of a person, ten of them sharing nothing but cc-land or a suite, and the row actually worth joining was
    thirteenth (review of #371). So a row is offered on two shared files, or on one that fewer than HUB live rows
    want — plus, either way, on being named by name.

    A row with NO shared file is offered when this brief names it by name, which is how a brief writes a
    dependency far more often than it writes a path; it sorts below every row that shares one."""
    d = board_of(repo)
    if d is None:
        return None
    tracks = {k: v for k, v in (d.get("tracks") or {}).items()
              if isinstance(v, dict) and (v.get("kind") or "track") == "track"}
    if track not in tracks:
        return []
    root = d.get("path") or ""
    index = tracked(root) if root else {}
    text = {k: brief_text(repo, k) for k in tracks}
    mine = named(text[track], root, index)
    # THE SAME RULE FOR A ROW NAMED BY NAME as for a file: "core/tests/*.sh by the email-receiver track — do not
    # touch them" names that track in order to stay out of its way, and joining is the one thing it must not
    # mean. `ready` reads the whole text for exactly the opposite reason and is right to.
    refs = referred(owning(text[track]), list(tracks)) - {track}
    if not mine and not refs:
        return []
    # NO NETWORK, like `refresh` and for the same reason: this runs inside `cc board add` and `--go`, and a `gh`
    # that is slow or logged out must never be what holds a dispatch up. A row no local ref places falls back to
    # what its own brief names — a guess, which is what an offer is made of anyway.
    byrow, _unplaced = held_files(d, skip={track}, net=False)
    wants = {k: set(byrow.get(k, ("", ()))[1]) | named(text[k], root, index)
             for k, v in tracks.items() if k != track and (v.get("status") or "") in LIVE}
    # A HUB IS COUNTED OVER THE WHOLE LIVE BOARD, not over the pair: whether a file is common ground is a fact
    # about the repo, and the asking row is counted among the rows wanting it because it does.
    hub = {f for f in mine if 1 + sum(1 for s in wants.values() if f in s) >= HUB}
    out = []
    for k in wants:
        v = tracks[k]
        shared = mine & wants[k]
        if k not in refs and (not shared or (len(shared) == 1 and shared <= hub)):
            continue
        st = v.get("status") or ""
        out.append((k, st, REACH["agent" if marked(v) else st].format(repo=repo, row=k), sorted(shared)))
    return sorted(out, key=lambda r: (-len(r[3]), ORDER.get(r[1], 9), r[0]))


def cmd_belongs(repo, track):
    """Print the offer, and NOTHING AT ALL when this work is nobody else's. Exit is always 0: this reports at a
    door, it does not gate one — two rows in the same file are often exactly what a person means, and a tool that
    refused them would be worked around within the day."""
    r = belongs(repo, track)
    if not r:
        return 0
    titles = {k: (v.get("title") or k) for k, v in ((board_of(repo) or {}).get("tracks") or {}).items()
              if isinstance(v, dict)}
    one = len(r) == 1
    print(f"{repo}/{track}: {len(r)} live row{'' if one else 's'} {'is' if one else 'are'} already on this work — "
          f"joining one saves a landing:")
    for name, st, how, shared in r[:JOIN_SHOW]:
        why = (", ".join(shared[:3]) + (f" and {len(shared) - 3} more" if len(shared) > 3 else "")
               if shared else "named in this brief")
        print(f"  {name} [{st}] — {why}. {how}")
        print(f"    cc board add {repo} {name} {shlex.quote(titles.get(name, name))} '<the ask>'")
    if len(r) > JOIN_SHOW:
        print(f"  …and {len(r) - JOIN_SHOW} more (cc board show {repo})")
    print("  a join is cheaper only while that row stays one concern (~5-8 files) — past that, two rows are right.")
    return 0


# ── the override ledger ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
OVERRIDES = os.path.join(STATE, "brief", "overrides.jsonl")


def fixture(for_=""):
    """A suite's dispatch is not a dispatch, and nothing it does is counted in the box's own ledgers — the rule
    cc-guard keeps for paging, kept here for the same reason: one landing's selftest.sh run wrote sixteen fixture
    rows into the real file (2026-09-04). The suite's repos are named _cctest…, its cwd sits under _cctest or
    _selfcheck, and it exports CC_NOTIFY_LOG_ONLY for its whole run: any one of those is enough.
    CC_STATE FIRST, AND IT WINS: a caller that pointed the ledger at a directory of its own is already keeping out
    of the box's record, and the row it writes there is the one it is asserting on — selftest.sh's forced-brief
    case redirects exactly so it can read its own override back."""
    if os.environ.get("CC_STATE"):
        return False
    if os.environ.get("CC_NOTIFY_LOG_ONLY"):
        return True
    try:
        here = os.getcwd()
    except OSError:
        here = ""
    return any(m in s for m in ("_cctest", "_selfcheck") for s in (for_ or "", here))


def record(path, what, for_, text):
    """A gate that did not hold is never silent: one JSON line per FORCED brief, countable with `wc -l` — a person
    decided that, and the line names the signal they went past. Unless a fixture wrote it."""
    if fixture(for_):
        return
    try:
        os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(path), exist_ok=True)
        with open(path, "a") as f:
            f.write(json.dumps({"ts": time.strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ", time.gmtime()), "what": what,
                                "for": for_, "head": " ".join(text.split())[:200]}) + "\n")
    except OSError:
        pass          # a record that cannot be written is not a reason to change what the gate decided


def main(a):
    if not a or a[0] in ("-h", "--help"):
        print(__doc__.rstrip())
        return 0
    cmd = a[0]
    if cmd == "check":
        rest, for_, src = a[1:], "", "-"
        i = 0
        while i < len(rest):
            if rest[i] == "--for" and i + 1 < len(rest):
                for_ = rest[i + 1]; i += 2
            else:
                src = rest[i]; i += 1
        if src == "-":
            text = sys.stdin.read()
        else:
            try:
                with open(src, errors="replace") as f:
                    text = f.read()
            except OSError as e:
                print(f"cc-brief check: {e}", file=sys.stderr)
                return 0        # unreadable is not prescription: never fail a dispatch on this tool's own fault
        return check(text, for_)
    if cmd == "refresh":
        if len(a) < 3:
            print("cc-brief refresh: needs <repo> <track>", file=sys.stderr)
            return 0
        return refresh(a[1], a[2])
    if cmd == "ready":
        rest = [x for x in a[1:] if x not in ("--net", "--no-net")]
        if not rest:
            print("cc-brief ready: needs <repo> [<track>…] [--no-net]", file=sys.stderr)
            return 2
        return cmd_ready(rest[0], rest[1:], net="--no-net" not in a[1:])
    if cmd == "belongs":
        if len(a) < 3:
            print("cc-brief belongs: needs <repo> <track>", file=sys.stderr)
            return 2
        return cmd_belongs(a[1], a[2])
    if cmd == "selfcheck":
        return selfcheck()
    print(f"cc-brief: unknown command '{cmd}'", file=sys.stderr)
    return 2


def selfcheck():
    import tempfile
    global BOARDS, STATE, OVERRIDES
    # THE LEDGER FIRST, before any case forces anything: it is resolved at import from the box's own state, and a
    # case below would otherwise append a fixture line to the real record.
    sc = tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="cc-brief-sc.")
    OVERRIDES = os.path.join(sc, "overrides.jsonl")
    os.environ.pop("CC_NOTIFY_LOG_ONLY", None)   # the ledger above is scratch: these cases must be able to write it
    p = f = 0

    def ok(name, cond):
        nonlocal p, f
        print(("ok   " if cond else "FAIL ") + name)
        p, f = p + (1 if cond else 0), f + (0 if cond else 1)

    # ── the check: prose passes, the three shapes do not ──
    prose = ("GOAL: a brief that prescribes how instead of what cannot be dispatched.\n\n"
             "WHY: the rule is written in two places and a session that read both still shipped a page of it.\n"
             "The board and git already know which files another track is holding, so nobody should type them.\n\n"
             "DONE: a brief carrying step lists is refused at dispatch, with the reason. Run cc board show to see.\n")
    ok("prose dispatches untouched — the owner's own message is most of a brief", findings(prose) == [])
    ok("one command named inside a sentence is not a finding",
       findings("Check with `cc board show <repo>` before you start, then decide for yourself.\n") == [])

    steps = ("Do this:\n"
             "- Add a --flag to core/bin/cc-foo\n"
             "- Edit core/bin/cc-bar so it passes the flag through\n"
             "- Run the suite and make sure it is green\n")
    sigs = [s for s, _, _ in findings(steps)]
    ok("three imperative bullets are a step list", sigs == ["step-list"])
    ok("three bullets of prose are not", findings(
        "- the owner caught it, not the box\n- the rule is in two places already\n- a third copy fails again\n") == [])

    criteria = ("DONE:\n1. a brief that says how is refused at dispatch, with the reason\n"
                "2. what is in flight comes from the board, not from somebody typing it\n"
                "3. the rule is not restated anywhere new\n")
    ok("a numbered list of done-criteria is what a brief is FOR — it passes", findings(criteria) == [])
    ok("the same list numbered but opening on edit verbs is a step list",
       [s for s, _, _ in findings("1. Add the check\n2. Wire it into both doors\n3. Run the suite\n")] == ["step-list"])
    ok("two imperative items are not yet a step list", findings("- Add the check\n- Wire it in\n") == [])

    # ── THE FOUR REJECTIONS THESE CASES ARE BUILT FROM ──
    # A small model used to read the prose and say what to cut. Across four dispatches it cut the sentence that
    # stated the OUTCOME, or the shape of the fixture, as method — and once that sentence was gone it cut the NEXT
    # one, so the brief could not be repaired by editing: five judge calls and six dispatch turns for one row.
    # It came out in #494 and the arithmetic above is all that judges a brief now. The sentences it cut are cases
    # here, each on its own fixture, so nothing put in its place may cut them again.
    for why, text in (
        ("an outcome — the reader gets the whole reply",
         "GOAL: a lost stream does not cost the reader a turn.\n\n"
         "A client whose stream was lost mid-turn can still get the whole reply.\n"),
        ("an outcome — the turn is not lost to the reader",
         "GOAL: a lost stream does not cost the reader a turn.\n\n"
         "DONE: a turn whose stream was lost mid-way is not lost to the reader.\n"),
        ("a boundary handing the design over",
         "GOAL: a lost stream does not cost the reader a turn.\n\n"
         "What the server keeps between a dropped stream and a re-attach is yours to design.\n"),
        ("a done clause naming what the PR body carries",
         "DONE: a keep means this track's PR carries the change and the doc rows, and in its body the figure\n"
         "before and projected after, the rule texts moved, and the date of the live after-score.\n"),
        ("a score defined as a before and an after figure",
         "Scores: before = the tool's own figure over the 7 days before; projected after = the same figure on a\n"
         "fresh session started on the branch, not an estimate from the diff. A keep is that figure down 20%.\n"),
    ):
        ok("the judge cut this and it passes: " + why, findings(text) == [])

    # AND THE REFUSAL THAT IS LEFT IS FIXABLE BY MAKING THE CUT IT QUOTED. What made those four unfixable was the
    # next sentence going once the first was cut; here the quoted lines are rewritten as the outcome they were
    # about, nothing else is touched, and that brief passes with no finding of its own.
    quoted = ("GOAL: a lost stream does not cost the reader a turn.\n\n"
              "- Add a buffer of the frames of the turn in flight\n"
              "- Wire a re-attach route that replays it\n"
              "- Change the client to ask for what it missed\n")
    repaired = ("GOAL: a lost stream does not cost the reader a turn.\n\n"
                "DONE: a client whose stream was lost mid-turn still gets the whole reply, and what the server\n"
                "keeps between the drop and the re-attach is yours to design.\n")
    ok("a brief refused for its step list passes once those lines are rewritten as the outcome",
       [s for s, _, _ in findings(quoted)] == ["step-list"] and findings(repaired) == [])

    # …AND A BRIEF THAT PRESCRIBES THE DESIGN IS STILL REFUSED. The first attempt of the dispatch above named
    # candidate designs and was cut for it, fairly; that is the shape this file still has to hold.
    design = ("GOAL: a lost stream does not cost the reader a turn.\n\n"
              "1. Add a ring buffer of the last 200 frames, keyed by turn id, to the stream module\n"
              "2. Wire a re-attach route that replays that buffer from the client's last seen frame\n"
              "3. Change the client to send its last frame id on reconnect\n")
    ok("a brief that prescribes the design is still refused",
       [s for s, _, _ in findings(design)] == ["step-list"])
    ok("a line of prose with no full stop is still prose, not a command",
       findings("Then run the suite and see\nand keep going until it is green\n") == [])

    fenced = "Then run:\n```\ngit checkout -b track/foo\nbash core/tests/selftest.sh\n```\n"
    fs = findings(fenced)
    ok("a fenced block of two commands is a command block", [s for s, _, _ in fs] == ["command-block"])
    ok("the block's finding quotes the commands it found",
       bool(fs) and any("selftest.sh" in line for line in fs[0][2]))
    ok("a fenced block with one command is not a script",
       findings("Like this:\n```\ncc board show <repo>\n```\n") == [])

    bare = "First:\ncc board add <repo> foo \"title\"\ncc <repo> foo --go \"go\"\n"
    ok("two bare command lines are a command run", [s for s, _, _ in findings(bare)] == ["command-run"])
    ok("one bare command line is not", [s for s, _, _ in findings("First:\ncc board show <repo>\n")] == [])
    ok("commands shown with what they printed are evidence, not instructions", findings(
        "What is actually there:\ngit ls-remote origin foo    -> 48fff51   (correct)\n"
        "gh pr view 9 --json headRefOid   -> 79fd93b   (STALE)\n") == [])
    ok("…while the same two commands single-spaced, with no result, are a script",
       [s for s, _, _ in findings("git ls-remote origin foo\ngh pr view 9 --json headRefOid\n")] == ["command-run"])

    # ── refuse vs flag: the force escape prints the SAME findings and lets it through ──
    import io, contextlib
    def run_check(text, force, machine_=False):
        for k in ("CC_BRIEF_FORCE", "CC_BRIEF_MACHINE"):
            os.environ.pop(k, None)
        if force:
            os.environ["CC_BRIEF_FORCE"] = "1"
        if machine_:
            os.environ["CC_BRIEF_MACHINE"] = "1"
        err = io.StringIO()
        with contextlib.redirect_stderr(err):
            rc = check(text, for_="r/t")
        for k in ("CC_BRIEF_FORCE", "CC_BRIEF_MACHINE"):
            os.environ.pop(k, None)
        return rc, err.getvalue()

    rc, err = run_check(steps, force=False)
    ok("a step list is refused at dispatch (exit 1)", rc == 1)
    ok("…and the refusal names the signal and quotes the line", "step-list" in err and "cc-foo" in err)
    ok("…and names the escape", "CC_BRIEF_FORCE=1" in err)
    rc2, err2 = run_check(steps, force=True)
    ok("forced, the same brief is FLAGGED and goes through (exit 0)", rc2 == 0 and "FLAGGED" in err2)
    ok("…and the flag still says what it found — force is never silence", "step-list" in err2)
    rc3, err3 = run_check(prose, force=False)
    ok("clean prose prints nothing at all", rc3 == 0 and err3 == "")

    # ── the block: computed from the board, and only the block is ever rewritten ──
    tmp = tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="cc-brief.")
    BOARDS, STATE = os.path.join(tmp, "boards"), os.path.join(tmp, "state")
    os.makedirs(BOARDS)
    def bounded(fn, secs=10):   # a read that hangs on the pipe (a plain open would, for good) fails THIS case, not the suite
        import threading
        box = []
        def go():
            try: box.append(fn())
            except Exception as e: box.append(e)
        t = threading.Thread(target=go, daemon=True); t.start(); t.join(secs)
        return box[0] if box else "hung"
    os.mkfifo(os.path.join(BOARDS, "pipe.json"))   # A PIPE PLANTED WHERE A BOARD SHOULD BE (review of #224): the read is cc-board's opener now, so it is no board, never a hang
    ok("a pipe planted where a board should be is 'nothing is known', not a hang", bounded(lambda: inflight("pipe")) is None)
    os.remove(os.path.join(BOARDS, "pipe.json"))
    repo = "selfcheckrepo"
    src = os.path.join(tmp, "repo")
    os.makedirs(src)

    def git(*args, **kw):
        return subprocess.run(["git", "-C", src, *args], capture_output=True, text=True,
                              env={**os.environ, "GIT_AUTHOR_NAME": "t", "GIT_AUTHOR_EMAIL": "t@t",
                                   "GIT_COMMITTER_NAME": "t", "GIT_COMMITTER_EMAIL": "t@t"}, **kw)

    subprocess.run(["git", "init", "-q", "-b", "main", src], capture_output=True)
    open(os.path.join(src, "held.txt"), "w").write("base\n")
    open(os.path.join(src, "other.txt"), "w").write("base\n")
    # …and, for the `ready` cases at the end: a file whose bare name is its own, and one basename two share.
    for d, fn in (("bin", "cc-fixture"), ("a", "dup.txt"), ("b", "dup.txt")):
        os.makedirs(os.path.join(src, d), exist_ok=True)
        open(os.path.join(src, d, fn), "w").write("base\n")
    for fn in ("pulled.txt", "wt.txt"):        # for the stale-`branch` cases: reached by PR ref and by worktree
        open(os.path.join(src, fn), "w").write("base\n")
    git("add", "-A"); git("commit", "-qm", "base")
    for br, fn in (("track/one", "held.txt"), ("track/two", "held.txt"), ("track/gone", "other.txt"),
                   ("track/tool", "bin/cc-fixture"), ("cut/elsewhere", "pulled.txt"), ("cut/checked-out", "wt.txt")):
        git("checkout", "-q", "-b", br, "main")
        open(os.path.join(src, fn), "a").write(br + "\n")
        git("add", "-A"); git("commit", "-qm", br)
    git("checkout", "-q", "main")
    # A ROW WHOSE `branch` FIELD IS STALE, both ways it is still readable without the network: somebody fetched
    # the PR, and the row's own worktree is sitting on the real branch whatever the board says.
    git("update-ref", "refs/pull/9/head", "cut/elsewhere")
    wt = os.path.join(tmp, "wt")
    git("worktree", "add", "-q", wt, "cut/checked-out")
    # A CHECKOUT THAT IS NOT THIS ONE, for the `worktree` guard: same branch name, another repository entirely.
    foreign = os.path.join(tmp, "foreign")
    subprocess.run(["git", "init", "-q", "-b", "cut/checked-out", foreign], capture_output=True)
    open(os.path.join(foreign, "wt.txt"), "w").write("theirs\n")
    subprocess.run(["git", "-C", foreign, "add", "-A"], capture_output=True)
    subprocess.run(["git", "-C", foreign, "commit", "-qm", "theirs"], capture_output=True,
                   env={**os.environ, "GIT_AUTHOR_NAME": "t", "GIT_AUTHOR_EMAIL": "t@t",
                        "GIT_COMMITTER_NAME": "t", "GIT_COMMITTER_EMAIL": "t@t"})

    def board(tracks):
        with open(os.path.join(BOARDS, repo + ".json"), "w") as fh:
            json.dump({"repo": repo, "path": src, "default_branch": "main", "tracks": tracks}, fh)

    board({"one": {"status": "review", "branch": "track/one"},
           "two": {"status": "running", "branch": "track/two"},
           "gone": {"status": "merged", "branch": "track/gone"}})
    blk = inflight(repo, exclude="two")
    ok("a live track's file is reported as held, with who holds it", "held.txt — one [review]" in blk)
    ok("…and the brief's own track is not reported against itself", "two [running]" not in blk)
    ok("a merged row holds nothing — its file is absent", "other.txt" not in blk)
    ok("the block is delimited so only it can ever be rewritten", blk.startswith(MARK_A) and blk.rstrip().endswith(MARK_B))

    board({"one": {"status": "review", "branch": "track/one"},
           "sess": {"status": "running", "branch": "track/gone", "kind": "session"}})
    blk2 = inflight(repo, exclude="")
    ok("a row a person marked kind=session is read and skipped, never reported as a holder",
       "held.txt" in blk2 and "sess" not in blk2 and "other.txt" not in blk2)

    board({"gone": {"status": "merged", "branch": "track/gone"}})
    ok("nothing held: no block, so a brief carries no line about nothing", inflight(repo, exclude="") == "")
    ok("an unreadable board is NOT the same answer as nothing held", inflight("no-such-board") is None)

    # rewrite: the typed brief survives verbatim, the stale block does not
    board({"one": {"status": "review", "branch": "track/one"}})
    td = os.path.join(STATE, repo, "mine")
    os.makedirs(td)
    typed = "GOAL: a goal a person typed.\n\nBOUNDARIES: nothing here is the machine's to touch.\n"
    task = os.path.join(td, "task.md")
    open(task, "w").write(typed + "\n" + MARK_A + "\nIN FLIGHT (from the board, 1999-01-01T00:00Z): stale.txt — ghost [running]\n" + MARK_B + "\n")
    refresh(repo, "mine")
    got = open(task).read()
    ok("refresh replaces the machine's stale block", "stale.txt" not in got and "held.txt" in got)
    ok("…and every line a person typed is still there, verbatim", typed.strip() in got)
    ok("…and there is exactly one block afterwards", got.count(MARK_A) == 1 and got.count(MARK_B) == 1)
    open(task, "w").write(typed)
    refresh(repo, "mine")
    ok("a brief with no block gets one appended", MARK_A in open(task).read() and typed.strip() in open(task).read())
    ok("the block is not checked as if a person typed it",
       findings(open(task).read()) == [] and "held.txt" in open(task).read())
    refresh(repo, "absent-track")
    ok("a track with no brief is not an error", True)

    # the block goes as it came: when the last holder finishes, the brief stops claiming the file is held
    board({"one": {"status": "merged", "branch": "track/one"}})
    refresh(repo, "mine")
    after = open(task).read()
    ok("the last holder finishing takes the block back out", MARK_A not in after and "held.txt" not in after)
    ok("…and the typed brief is still whole", typed.strip() in after)
    # ITS OWN FIXTURE, and a pure one: put the block in and take it out again, and the writer's text — including a
    # gap they left — must come back byte for byte. The tool tidying a brief it did not write is the thing to catch.
    theirs = "GOAL: a thing.\n\n\n\nwith a gap the writer left.\n"
    merge = _board().merge
    ok("adding the block and taking it out again returns the typed brief byte for byte",
       merge(merge(theirs, MARK_A + "\nIN FLIGHT: x\n" + MARK_B + "\n"), "") == theirs)

    open(task, "w").write(typed + "\n" + MARK_A + "\nIN FLIGHT: something\n" + MARK_B + "\n")
    refresh("no-such-board", "mine")
    ok("a board that cannot be read leaves the block alone rather than deleting it",
       MARK_A in open(task).read())

    # ── ready: which queued rows nothing in flight is holding, and which of them are one builder's work ──
    def brief_of(track, text):
        d = os.path.join(STATE, repo, track)
        os.makedirs(d, exist_ok=True)
        open(os.path.join(d, "task.md"), "w").write(text)

    def say(*only):
        """cmd_ready as `main` would reach it, so the flag parsing is under test too, not just the arithmetic."""
        buf = io.StringIO()
        with contextlib.redirect_stdout(buf):
            rc = main(["ready", repo, *only])
        return rc, buf.getvalue()

    idx = tracked(src)
    ok("a bare name exactly one tracked file carries IS that file — briefs write `cc-fixture`, not its path",
       named("GOAL: cc-fixture answers the wrong question.\n", src, idx) == {"bin/cc-fixture"})
    ok("…and a basename two directories share names neither of them, so it is not read as a file at all",
       named("GOAL: dup.txt is wrong.\n", src, idx) == set())
    ok("a whole path counts when it resolves in the checkout, and is prose when it does not",
       named("GOAL: bin/cc-fixture, not bin/cc-nothing.\n", src, idx) == {"bin/cc-fixture"})
    ok("an ordinary word is never a file, however many of them a brief has",
       named("GOAL: the box says what could be running, and nobody has to notice by hand.\n", src, idx) == set())
    # A BOUNDARY IS SPELT LIKE A TARGET, and read as one it made ten rows collide on a file every brief warns
    # about (review of #371). The clause that says a file is somebody else's is dropped, and only that clause.
    ok("A FILE NAMED IN ORDER TO SAY IT WILL NOT BE TOUCHED IS NOT THIS ROW'S — and the target beside it still is",
       named("GOAL: held.txt is wrong.\nBOUNDARIES: other.txt is held by PR #9. Never edits bin/cc-fixture.\n",
             src, idx) == {"held.txt"})
    ok("…and the em-dash a brief hangs its boundary off does not end the clause, or the path would come straight "
       "back", named("GOAL: a thing.\nBOUNDARIES: wt.txt, pulled.txt — do not touch them.\n", src, idx) == set())
    ok("…while a boundary in the NEXT sentence cannot eat the one before it",
       named("GOAL: held.txt is wrong. Leave other.txt alone.\n", src, idx) == {"held.txt"})
    # A BOUNDARY GOVERNS ITS CLAUSE AND NOT THE SENTENCE. Prose here is compound, so dropping the sentence took
    # the row's real target out with the boundary and under-reported what it owns (re-review of #371).
    ok("a sentence that says what this row WILL write and what it must stay off keeps the first and drops the "
       "second — one clause each",
       named("GOAL: this row will edit bin/cc-fixture, but do not touch held.txt -- that stays with the other "
             "track.\n", src, idx) == {"bin/cc-fixture"})
    ok("…and an ownership clause joined to a boundary one by `and` survives it, which is how a BOUNDARIES line "
       "is usually written",
       named("BOUNDARIES: held.txt is held by PR #9 and other.txt is this row's to rewrite.\n",
             src, idx) == {"other.txt"})
    ok("…but a boundary's own comma LIST is one boundary, not a target after the first name",
       named("GOAL: never edits held.txt, other.txt, bin/cc-fixture or the approval list.\n", src, idx) == set())

    FRESH = time.strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ", time.gmtime())
    board({"one": {"status": "review", "branch": "track/one"},
           "q1": {"status": "queued"}, "q2": {"status": "queued"}, "q3": {"status": "queued"},
           "sat": {"status": "queued", "held": "the owner has not decided yet"},
           "mine": {"status": "queued", "agent": "@1:7", "agent_at": FRESH, "branch": "track/tool"}})
    brief_of("q1", "GOAL: what held.txt says is wrong.\n")
    brief_of("q2", "GOAL: other.txt says the wrong thing.\n")
    brief_of("q3", "GOAL: other.txt again, from the other end.\n")
    brief_of("sat", "GOAL: other.txt, but nobody is to touch it yet.\n")
    brief_of("mine", "GOAL: bin/cc-fixture, and a subagent is already on it.\n")
    free, groups, weighed, unplaced, blind = ready(repo)
    ok("THE ROWS THAT COULD START NOW ARE NAMED — nothing in flight is holding a file their briefs name",
       free == ["q2", "q3"])
    ok("…and a queued row whose brief names a file a live track is holding is not one of them", "q1" not in free)
    ok("a row a person is sitting on and a row a live session marked as its own are not even weighed",
       weighed == 3 and "sat" not in free and "mine" not in free)
    ok("two free rows naming the SAME file are ONE builder's work, and come out grouped by that file",
       groups == [(["other.txt"], ["q2", "q3"])])
    rc, out = say()
    ok("the printed answer carries both halves — how many could start, and which belong together",
       rc == 0 and "2 of 3 queued rows could start now" in out and "one builder for other.txt: q2, q3" in out)

    board({"a1": {"status": "queued"}, "a2": {"status": "queued"}, "a3": {"status": "queued"}})
    brief_of("a1", "GOAL: held.txt.\n")
    brief_of("a2", "GOAL: held.txt, and other.txt with it.\n")
    brief_of("a3", "GOAL: other.txt.\n")
    ok("the join is transitive: a shares with b and b with c, so all three are one builder's and not two workers'",
       ready(repo)[1] == [(["held.txt", "other.txt"], ["a1", "a2", "a3"])])
    ok("a caller that has already decided which rows to weigh gets exactly those weighed",
       ready(repo, only=["a1"])[2] == 1)

    board({"tool": {"status": "running", "branch": "track/tool"}, "q4": {"status": "queued"}})
    brief_of("q4", "GOAL: cc-fixture answers the wrong question.\n")
    ok("a row naming its file the way a brief actually writes it — the bare name — still sees the collision",
       ready(repo)[0] == [])

    board({"one": {"status": "review", "branch": "track/one"}, "q2": {"status": "queued"}})
    brief_of("q2", "GOAL: other.txt says the wrong thing.\n\n" + MARK_A +
             "\nIN FLIGHT (from the board, 1999-01-01T00:00Z):\n  held.txt — one [review]\n" + MARK_B + "\n")
    ok("the IN FLIGHT block is this tool's own writing, so a brief carrying one does not collide with it",
       ready(repo)[0] == ["q2"])

    # ── A STALE `branch` FIELD IS NORMAL, and reading it as the whole answer is how a live PR held nothing at
    # all: the ref did not resolve, the diff came back empty, and every queued row sharing that PR's files read
    # as free (review of #367). Three ways the work is still found without the network, then the honest UNKNOWN.
    ok("the number is read off a `pr` field written either way — bare, or as the whole URL",
       pr_number("9") == "9" and pr_number("https://example.invalid/x/y/pull/364") == "364")
    board({"wt": {"status": "review", "branch": "track/long-gone", "worktree": wt}, "q6": {"status": "queued"}})
    brief_of("q6", "GOAL: wt.txt is wrong.\n")
    byrow, unplaced = held_files(board_of(repo), skip={"q6"})
    ok("a row whose branch field is stale is placed by its own WORKTREE, which is on the real branch",
       byrow.get("wt") == ("review", ["wt.txt"]) and unplaced == [])
    ok("…so the queued row that shares that file is not offered as free", ready(repo)[0] == [])

    board({"pr9": {"status": "review", "branch": "track/long-gone", "pr": "9"}, "q7": {"status": "queued"}})
    brief_of("q7", "GOAL: pulled.txt is wrong.\n")
    byrow, unplaced = held_files(board_of(repo), skip={"q7"})
    ok("a row with no worktree either is placed by its PR, from a refs/pull ref somebody fetched — no network",
       byrow.get("pr9") == ("review", ["pulled.txt"]) and unplaced == [])
    ok("…and that PR's files are held against the queued row that names one", ready(repo)[0] == [])

    # ── A ROW NOTHING PLACES WITHHOLDS THE ANSWER RATHER THAN QUALIFYING IT. Its files are unknown, and FREE IS
    # A CLAIM OF SAFETY: the row this was measured on holds two files its brief never spells, so a queued row
    # naming one of them came back free with a general note that some row was unread as the only warning
    # (re-review of #367). The guess its brief gives can take a row OUT of the free list; it can never put one in.
    board({"lost": {"status": "review", "branch": "track/long-gone", "pr": "5"}, "q8": {"status": "queued"},
           "q9": {"status": "queued"}})
    brief_of("lost", "GOAL: pulled.txt is wrong, and nothing here says where the work is.\n")
    brief_of("q8", "GOAL: pulled.txt from the other end.\n")
    brief_of("q9", "GOAL: wt.txt, which nobody is on.\n")
    free, groups, weighed, unplaced, blind = ready(repo, net=False)
    ok("a row IN FLIGHT with a PR that nothing places is UNKNOWN, never 'holds nothing'",
       unplaced == ["lost"] and blind == [])
    ok("…and NO row is called free while it is there — not the one that shares its files, nor the one that does "
       "not: a brief's paths are a guess, and free is a claim of safety",
       free == [] and "q9" not in free)
    rc, out = say("--no-net")
    ok("…and the answer names it with its goal line, so a person can go and place it by hand",
       "none can be called free" in out and "lost (read from its brief only) — GOAL: pulled.txt is wrong" in out)
    ok("…and says what would settle it, and gives no count that reads as room",
       "the branch the work is really on" in out and "could start now" not in out)

    board({"blind": {"status": "review", "branch": "track/long-gone", "pr": "5"}, "qa": {"status": "queued"}})
    brief_of("blind", "GOAL: a member session's work reaches the far end without a person.\n\n"
                      "WHY: it is described here in prose and no filename appears anywhere in it.\n")
    brief_of("qa", "GOAL: held.txt is wrong.\n")
    free, groups, weighed, unplaced, blind = ready(repo, net=False)
    ok("a row whose OWN BRIEF names no file either is blind — nothing at all is known about what it holds",
       blind == ["blind"] and unplaced == ["blind"] and free == [])
    rc, out = say("--no-net")
    ok("…and the answer says which of the two it is, so the reader knows there is nothing to go on",
       "blind (brief names no file either) — GOAL: a member session's work reaches" in out)
    board({"blind": {"status": "review", "branch": "track/long-gone", "pr": "5", "worktree": src},
           "qa": {"status": "queued"}})
    ok("…and placing it — here by a worktree that IS this repo's — brings the whole answer back",
       ready(repo, net=False) == (["qa"], [], 1, [], []))
    board({"blind": {"status": "review", "branch": "track/long-gone", "pr": "5", "worktree": tmp},
           "qa": {"status": "queued"}})
    ok("a `worktree` field is as stale as a `branch` one: a directory that is not a checkout places nothing",
       ready(repo, net=False)[3] == ["blind"])
    board({"blind": {"status": "review", "branch": "track/long-gone", "pr": "5", "worktree": foreign},
           "qa": {"status": "queued"}})
    ok("…and a checkout that is ANOTHER repository's places nothing either, whatever branch it is on",
       ready(repo, net=False)[3] == ["blind"])

    # …and GitHub is the last place a row can be placed, which is why it is asked BY DEFAULT: withholding is only
    # worth anything if the ordinary run has something to withhold about. `--no-net` is for a run that must not
    # leave the box, and it answers less often — that is the trade, and both halves are here.
    gbin = os.path.join(tmp, "gbin")
    os.makedirs(gbin, exist_ok=True)
    open(os.path.join(gbin, "gh"), "w").write("#!/bin/sh\nprintf '%s\\n' held.txt\n")
    os.chmod(os.path.join(gbin, "gh"), 0o755)
    was_path = os.environ["PATH"]
    os.environ["PATH"] = gbin + os.pathsep + was_path
    board({"lost": {"status": "review", "branch": "track/long-gone", "pr": "https://example.invalid/x/pull/77"},
           "qn": {"status": "queued"}, "qz": {"status": "queued"}})
    brief_of("lost", "GOAL: nothing here says where the work is.\n")
    brief_of("qn", "GOAL: held.txt is wrong.\n")
    brief_of("qz", "GOAL: other.txt is wrong.\n")
    ok("GitHub places a row no local ref could, and the answer comes back whole — the PR's files held, the row "
       "that shares none of them free",
       ready(repo) == (["qz"], [], 2, [], []))
    ok("…and --no-net makes no call at all: the row is unplaced, and nothing is called free",
       ready(repo, net=False) == ([], [], 2, ["lost"], ["lost"]))
    os.environ["PATH"] = was_path

    board({"never": {"status": "queued", "branch": "track/long-gone"}, "q8": {"status": "queued"}})
    ok("a row nobody ever dispatched — no ref, no PR, no mark — holds nothing and is not unknown either",
       ready(repo, only=["q8"]) == (["q8"], [], 1, [], []))
    # ── A ROW WITH NO BRANCH ANYWHERE HOLDS NOTHING, WHATEVER ITS STATUS SAYS (raised-one-branchless-row-makes-
    # every-queued-row-unplaceable, 2026-09-12): a project row an orch holds — `running`, its `agent` mark warm,
    # a `branch` and `worktree` nothing resolves, no PR — made all 23 queued rows "unplaceable", every pulse.
    stamp = time.strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ", time.gmtime())
    board({"project": {"status": "running", "branch": "track/long-gone", "worktree": os.path.join(tmp, "nowhere"),
                       "agent": "@1:2", "agent_at": stamp},
           "q8": {"status": "queued"}, "q9": {"status": "queued"}})
    brief_of("project", "GOAL: a knowledge library for the box; its code work runs in tracks of its own.\n")
    brief_of("q8", "GOAL: pulled.txt from the other end.\n")
    brief_of("q9", "GOAL: wt.txt, which nobody is on.\n")
    byrow, unplaced = held_files(board_of(repo), skip={"q8", "q9"}, net=False)
    ok("a running row an orch holds, with no ref, no worktree and no PR, holds nothing — it is not unplaced",
       byrow.get("project") == ("running", []) and unplaced == [])
    ok("…so the queued rows are called free, and the answer is not withheld for it",
       ready(repo, net=False) == (["q8", "q9"], [], 2, [], []))
    # …and the same row WITH a PR is the #367 case again: its work is somewhere, and the answer waits on it.
    board({"project": {"status": "running", "branch": "track/long-gone", "pr": "5"}, "q8": {"status": "queued"}})
    ok("…while the same row with a PR nothing local places is still unknown, as before",
       ready(repo, net=False)[3] == ["project"])
    # ── THE WORKTREE GIT LISTS UNDER THE ROW'S OWN NAME places it when both board fields are stale or empty.
    named_wt = os.path.join(tmp, "by-name")
    git("worktree", "add", "-q", "-b", "cut/by-name", named_wt, "cut/checked-out")
    board({"by-name": {"status": "running", "branch": "track/long-gone"}, "qb": {"status": "queued"}})
    brief_of("qb", "GOAL: wt.txt is wrong.\n")
    byrow, unplaced = held_files(board_of(repo), skip={"qb"}, net=False)
    ok("a row whose branch field is stale and whose worktree field is empty is placed by the worktree git lists "
       "under its own name", byrow.get("by-name") == ("running", ["wt.txt"]) and unplaced == [])
    ok("…and holds its file against the queued row that names it", ready(repo, net=False)[0] == [])
    git("worktree", "remove", "--force", named_wt)
    # ── A ROW INSIDE A LIVE BUNDLE IS BEING WRITTEN, whoever asks: cc-bundle's `bundle` field on a member names
    # the bundle row, and the member stays `queued` so that bundle's PR can close it.
    board({"bundle-x": {"status": "running", "branch": "track/long-gone"},
           "m1": {"status": "queued", "bundle": "bundle-x"}, "m2": {"status": "queued", "bundle": "bundle-x"},
           "solo": {"status": "queued"}})
    for k in ("m1", "m2", "solo"):
        brief_of(k, "GOAL: other.txt is wrong.\n")
    ok("a queued row inside a live bundle is not a candidate, even when it is named",
       sorted(candidates(board_of(repo))) == ["solo"] and candidates(board_of(repo), only=["m1", "solo"]).keys() == {"solo"})
    board({"bundle-x": {"status": "done", "branch": "track/long-gone"},
           "m1": {"status": "queued", "bundle": "bundle-x"}, "solo": {"status": "queued"}})
    ok("…and comes back to the pool once the bundle row is no longer live",
       sorted(candidates(board_of(repo))) == ["m1", "solo"])

    # ── A DEPENDENCY NAMED BY TRACK IS A COLLISION. Briefs say "it goes after <row> — same file" and never spell
    # the file, so the row read as free while that row's PR held it.
    board({"the-holder-row": {"status": "review", "branch": "track/one"},
           "q10": {"status": "queued"}, "q11": {"status": "queued"}})
    brief_of("q10", "GOAL: a thing.\n\nBOUNDARIES: this goes after the-holder-row - it builds on it and cannot "
                    "be written in parallel with it.\n")
    brief_of("q11", "GOAL: a thing that stands on its own and names nobody.\n")
    free, groups, weighed, unplaced, blind = ready(repo)
    ok("a brief that names another ROW inherits that row's held files, spelled out or not", free == ["q11"])
    board({"q12": {"status": "queued"}, "q13": {"status": "queued"}})
    brief_of("q12", "GOAL: this follows q13 and lands on top of it.\n")
    brief_of("q13", "GOAL: other.txt says the wrong thing.\n")
    ok("…and two QUEUED rows joined that way are one builder's work, by the file only one of them named",
       ready(repo)[1] == [(["other.txt"], ["q12", "q13"])])

    # THE SAME SENTENCE HAS TO ANSWER BOTH WAYS at this end too, where own[] is what a queued row is weighed on:
    # the file it names to stay OFF must not hold it back, and the file the same sentence says it will write must.
    brief_of("compound", "GOAL: this row will edit other.txt, but do not touch held.txt "
                         "— that stays with the other track.\n")
    board({"holds-held": {"status": "review", "branch": "track/one"}, "compound": {"status": "queued"}})
    off = ready(repo)[0]
    board({"holds-other": {"status": "review", "branch": "track/gone"}, "compound": {"status": "queued"}})
    ok("a row is free of the file its own brief named only to STAY OFF it, and still held by the file the same "
       "compound sentence says it will write", off == ["compound"] and ready(repo)[0] == [])

    board({"one": {"status": "review", "branch": "track/one"}, "q1": {"status": "queued"}})
    ok("NOTHING COULD START IS SILENCE — not a count, not a heading, not a line", say() == (0, ""))
    board({"one": {"status": "merged", "branch": "track/one"}})
    ok("…and so is a board with no row waiting at all", say() == (0, ""))
    buf = io.StringIO()
    with contextlib.redirect_stdout(buf):
        rc = cmd_ready("no-such-board", ())
    ok("a board that cannot be READ is not the same answer as nothing, and neither is printed as the other",
       ready("no-such-board") is None and rc == 0 and buf.getvalue() == "")

    # ── belongs: a new ask joins the row already in that code, instead of opening a landing of its own ──
    def offer(track):
        """cmd_belongs as `main` reaches it, so the argument handling is under test with the arithmetic."""
        buf = io.StringIO()
        with contextlib.redirect_stdout(buf):
            rc = main(["belongs", repo, track])
        return rc, buf.getvalue()

    board({"already-on-it": {"status": "review", "branch": "track/one", "title": "held.txt, from the far end"},
           "elsewhere": {"status": "running", "branch": "track/gone"},
           "landed": {"status": "merged", "branch": "track/two"},
           "new": {"status": "queued"}, "lone": {"status": "queued"}})
    brief_of("new", "GOAL: held.txt is wrong in one more way.\n")
    brief_of("lone", "GOAL: wt.txt, which nobody at all is on.\n")
    got = belongs(repo, "new")
    ok("A NEW ASK IN A FILE A LIVE ROW IS WRITING IS TOLD WHICH ROW, and told nothing about the rest of the board",
       [(r[0], r[1], r[3]) for r in got] == [("already-on-it", "review", ["held.txt"])])
    rc, out = offer("new")
    ok("…and the offer is a command a person can run, against the row's own title, not a description of one",
       rc == 0 and "1 live row is already on this work" in out
       and "cc board add selfcheckrepo already-on-it 'held.txt, from the far end' '<the ask>'" in out)
    ok("…and it says the row's PR is open, which is what decides whether the ask can still go in",
       "already-on-it [review] — held.txt." in out and "its PR is open" in out)
    ok("A ROW THAT OVERLAPS NOTHING IS LEFT ALONE — no offer, no heading, no line at all", offer("lone") == (0, ""))
    ok("a merged row is not offered: its landing is spent, and joining it buys nothing",
       all(r[0] != "landed" for r in got))

    board({"a-queued-one": {"status": "queued", "branch": "track/two"},
           "z-pr-open": {"status": "review", "branch": "track/one"}, "new": {"status": "queued"}})
    brief_of("new", "GOAL: held.txt and other.txt are both wrong.\n")
    brief_of("a-queued-one", "GOAL: held.txt from a third angle, and other.txt with it.\n")
    brief_of("z-pr-open", "GOAL: other.txt as well as held.txt.\n")
    ok("the row furthest along is offered first: an open PR is a landing already committed to, and two QUEUED "
       "rows sharing a file are what `ready` groups anyway",
       [r[0] for r in belongs(repo, "new")] == ["z-pr-open", "a-queued-one"])
    brief_of("new", "GOAL: held.txt is wrong in one more way.\n")

    # ── ONE SHARED FILE IS ONLY EVIDENCE WHILE IT IS THE PAIR'S. Offering on any single file put sixteen rows in
    # front of a person, ten of them sharing nothing but cc-land or a suite, and buried the row worth joining. ──
    board({"hub-a": {"status": "review", "branch": "track/one"},
           "hub-b": {"status": "running", "branch": "track/two"}, "new": {"status": "queued"}})
    ok("A SINGLE SHARED FILE THE REST OF THE LIVE BOARD ALSO WANTS IS COMMON GROUND, not a concern in common: an "
       "offer resting on it alone is not made", belongs(repo, "new") == [])
    board({"hub-a": {"status": "review", "branch": "track/one"}, "new": {"status": "queued"}})
    ok("…while that same one file shared with ONE other row is exactly the pair worth offering",
       [r[0] for r in belongs(repo, "new")] == ["hub-a"])
    board({"hub-a": {"status": "review", "branch": "track/one"},
           "hub-b": {"status": "running", "branch": "track/two"},
           "the-real-pair": {"status": "running", "branch": "track/tool"}, "new": {"status": "queued"}})
    brief_of("new", "GOAL: held.txt and bin/cc-fixture are both wrong.\n")
    brief_of("the-real-pair", "GOAL: held.txt, and bin/cc-fixture with it.\n")
    ok("…and a SECOND shared file clears the floor however common the first is — which is the row a person was "
       "looking for", [r[0] for r in belongs(repo, "new")] == ["the-real-pair"])

    # A BOUNDARY IS NOT OWNERSHIP, the same rule `named` keeps: a brief spelling a file it must stay out of was
    # read as writing it, and the row actually holding that file was offered as one to lump the ask onto.
    board({"in-my-file": {"status": "review", "branch": "track/one"},
           "in-the-one-i-must-not": {"status": "running", "branch": "track/gone"}, "new": {"status": "queued"}})
    brief_of("new", "GOAL: held.txt is wrong in one more way.\n"
                    "BOUNDARIES: other.txt is held by in-the-one-i-must-not — do not touch it.\n")
    ok("A ROW WHOSE FILE THIS BRIEF NAMES ONLY TO STAY OUT OF IT IS NOT OFFERED, by file or by its own name, and "
       "the row holding what this one is actually writing still is",
       [r[0] for r in belongs(repo, "new")] == ["in-my-file"])
    brief_of("new", "GOAL: held.txt is wrong in one more way.\n")

    # THE ASYMMETRY THE ROW EXISTS FOR: a session's own subagent takes the ask mid-run and nothing on the box can
    # see it, so the mark is the only sign; a headless worker cannot be reached at all and gets its brief instead.
    board({"in-a-subagent": {"status": "running", "branch": "track/one", "agent": "@1:7", "agent_at": FRESH},
           "new": {"status": "queued"}})
    rc, out = offer("new")
    ok("a row a session is running in its own subagent is offered as one to patch through that session",
       "has it in a subagent, which takes the ask mid-run" in out)
    board({"a-worker": {"status": "running", "branch": "track/one"}, "new": {"status": "queued"}})
    rc, out = offer("new")
    ok("…and a row with no mark says both routes, because the board cannot tell a window from a headless worker",
       "cc selfcheckrepo a-worker --say" in out and "reads its brief at the next iteration" in out)

    # A DEPENDENCY NAMED BY TRACK IS THE SAME OFFER, and briefs write one far more often than they write a path.
    board({"the-other-row": {"status": "running", "branch": "track/gone"}, "new": {"status": "queued"}})
    brief_of("new", "GOAL: a thing.\n\nBOUNDARIES: this builds on the-other-row and cannot be written beside it.\n")
    rc, out = offer("new")
    ok("a brief that names another live ROW is offered that row, with no file spelled anywhere in either",
       "the-other-row [running] — named in this brief" in out)

    # GENEROUS WHERE `ready` IS CAREFUL: a row no ref places still offers what its own brief names, because this
    # is a suggestion a person weighs and not a claim that anything is safe to start.
    board({"unplaced": {"status": "review", "branch": "track/long-gone"}, "new": {"status": "queued"}})
    brief_of("unplaced", "GOAL: held.txt is being rewritten here.\n")
    brief_of("new", "GOAL: held.txt is wrong in one more way.\n")
    ok("a row nothing places is still offered, on the files its own brief names",
       [r[0] for r in belongs(repo, "new")] == ["unplaced"])

    board({"one": {"status": "review", "branch": "track/one"}, "new": {"status": "queued"}})
    brief_of("new", "GOAL: other.txt.\n\n" + MARK_A +
             "\nIN FLIGHT (from the board, 1999-01-01T00:00Z):\n  held.txt — one [review]\n" + MARK_B + "\n")
    ok("the IN FLIGHT block is this tool's own writing, so a brief carrying one is not offered its own block back",
       belongs(repo, "new") == [])
    board({"sess": {"status": "running", "branch": "track/one", "kind": "session"}, "new": {"status": "queued"}})
    brief_of("new", "GOAL: held.txt is wrong in one more way.\n")
    ok("a row a person marked kind=session holds no work to join, so it is never offered",
       belongs(repo, "new") == [])
    ok("a track the board does not carry is nothing to offer against, and is not an error",
       belongs(repo, "no-such-track") == [])
    buf = io.StringIO()
    with contextlib.redirect_stdout(buf):
        rc = cmd_belongs("no-such-board", "new")
    ok("a board that cannot be read says nothing and offers nothing, and is not the same answer as no overlap",
       belongs("no-such-board", "new") is None and rc == 0 and buf.getvalue() == "")

    # ── the override ledger: force is flag, never silence, and a machine's own row is not a decision ──
    def rows(path):
        return [json.loads(l) for l in open(path) if l.strip()]
    open(OVERRIDES, "w").close()
    run_check(steps, force=True)
    lines = rows(OVERRIDES)
    ok("forcing past the check is recorded — one countable line, named by signal, naming the brief",
       len(lines) == 1 and lines[0]["what"] == "step-list" and lines[0]["for"] == "r/t" and "cc-foo" in lines[0]["head"])
    open(OVERRIDES, "w").close()
    run_check(prose, force=False)
    ok("a clean brief records nothing — the ledger counts overrides, not dispatches", rows(OVERRIDES) == [])
    # A MACHINE ROW IS NOT AN OVERRIDE. the landing writes its own repair brief out of a review it bought; counting
    # those as deliberate overrides would make `wc -l` on the ledger a number about nobody. Both halves here: the
    # same text is still refused without the flag, so the case cannot pass by the text being harmless.
    ok("without the flag that same row is still refused — the flag is doing the work", run_check(steps, force=False)[0] == 1)
    rc, err = run_check(steps, force=False, machine_=True)
    ok("CC_BRIEF_MACHINE=1: the check lets a machine's own row past, and says nothing", rc == 0 and err == "")
    ok("…and wrote no line: the ledger stays a count of decisions people made", rows(OVERRIDES) == [])
    # ── A SUITE'S DISPATCH IS NOT COUNTED. selftest.sh forces briefs of its own through every door, and one
    # landing's run wrote sixteen fixture rows into the real ledger (2026-09-04).
    os.environ["CC_NOTIFY_LOG_ONLY"] = "1"
    run_check(steps, force=True)
    ok("under the suite's own flag (CC_NOTIFY_LOG_ONLY) a forced brief writes no row", rows(OVERRIDES) == [])
    os.environ["CC_STATE"] = sc
    run_check(steps, force=True)
    ok("…but a ledger the caller pointed elsewhere (CC_STATE) is that caller's to write, flag or not",
       len(rows(OVERRIDES)) == 1)
    os.environ.pop("CC_STATE", None)
    os.environ.pop("CC_NOTIFY_LOG_ONLY", None)
    open(OVERRIDES, "w").close()
    os.environ["CC_BRIEF_FORCE"] = "1"
    with contextlib.redirect_stderr(io.StringIO()):
        rc = check(steps, for_="_cctest9/w1")
    os.environ.pop("CC_BRIEF_FORCE", None)
    ok("…nor does one for a _cctest repo, whatever the env — and it still goes (0)", rc == 0 and rows(OVERRIDES) == [])
    run_check(steps, force=True)
    ok("…while a real dispatch after them is still counted", len(rows(OVERRIDES)) == 1)

    print(f"\ncc-brief selfcheck: {p} passed, {f} failed")
    return 1 if f else 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main(sys.argv[1:]))
